{"question": "A 26-year-old G1P0 woman presents to her primary care physician\u2019s office with feelings of anxiety and trouble with sleep. She finds it difficult initiating sleep, occasionally has palpitations, and feels fatigued. She denies having similar symptoms in the past or starting any new medications or illicit drugs. She is currently 10 weeks pregnant and is closely followed by her obstetrician. Her temperature is 98.6\u00b0F (37\u00b0C), blood pressure is 125/70 mmHg, pulse is 105/min, and respirations are 18/min. On physical exam, the patient is mildly diaphoretic. The skin is warm and the thyroid gland is diffusely enlarged with thyroid bruits. Laboratory studies are significant for a thyroid-stimulating hormone level of 0.01 \u00b5U/mL (normal is 0.5-5.0 \u00b5U/mL) and an elevated free thyroxine (FT4) that is inappropriate for her pregnancy. Which of the following is the best treatment option for this patient?", "options": {"A": "Methimazole", "B": "Radioiodine therapy", "C": "Thyroidectomy", "D": "Observation", "E": "Propylthiouracil"}, "pred_answer": "E", "gold_answer": "E", "question_domains": ["Endocrinology", "Obstetrics", "Internal Medicine", "Psychiatry", "Cardiology"], "option_domains": ["Endocrinology", "Obstetrics"], "question_analyses": {"Endocrinology": "The patient described is a 26-year-old pregnant woman at 10 weeks gestation who is presenting with symptoms of anxiety, sleep disturbances, palpitations, and fatigue. These symptoms, combined with her physical exam findings of a warm skin, diaphoresis, and a diffusely enlarged thyroid gland with thyroid bruits, along with laboratory results showing a suppressed thyroid-stimulating hormone (TSH) level and elevated free thyroxine (FT4), strongly suggest a diagnosis of hyperthyroidism, specifically Graves' disease, which is the most common cause of hyperthyroidism during pregnancy. The most alarming aspects of this scenario are:\n\n1. **Thyroid Function Abnormalities**: The patient's TSH lev